claimed: okay-script-check -- mdoc-style output-comparison literate testing (filed in BACKLOG since okay-script core landed). A block's expected stdout written inline in the markdown, checked against Result.stdout from a real run() -- run already captures everything needed; the missing piece is the markdown convention for "expected output" and the comparison step. Plan: a new fenced tag ```stdout immediately following a ```scala block names the output that block (and everything before it, since it is one flat program) should have produced BY THAT POINT. ScalaScript.check(markdown, classpath): CheckResult runs the document once via run() (no synthesis changes -- purely a host-side, additive function) and verifies each ```stdout chunk, in document order, appears as an IN-ORDER substring of the actual stdout (each match search starts after the previous chunk's match end) -- proving the checkpoints happened in the right relative sequence without needing precise incremental partitioning of one continuous stdout capture.
